<para>b. \(Q(1)=16\), \(Q(n)=Q(n−1)−5\) for \(n\geq2\)</para>
<title>Student Facing Extension</title>
<title>Are you ready for more?</title>
<para>Make a visual pattern (for example, using dots or boxes), starting with Step 0, so the pattern for Step \(n\)
  contains \(n^2+3n+3\) dots.</para>
<title> Extension Student Response</title>
<para>For example: an \(n+1\) by \(n+2\) rectangle with one additional dot</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4fdaecf324da14ed043e2f783d8bdbaee287e8cf"/></media></figure></para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>After students have completed their work, share the correct answers and ask students to discuss the process of
  representing the sequences in different ways. Display the cards for all to see throughout the discussion. Some
  students may be surprised to see the different structures of the cards even though they were about the same topic.
  Allow time for students to share what questions they found helpful for solving their problem card and how they came up
  with them.</para>
<para>Highlight connections between the representations. For example, a recursive definition might include \(f(1)=16\),
  which corresponds to an entry in a table and the point \((1,16)\) on a graph.</para>
<title>4.17.3: Self Check </title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their
    understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Which of the following recursive definitions is represented by the graph below?</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/5a18ffb1e1cf2d053ce1861f58f826d34d5d7dbf"/></media></figure></para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">

  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        Answers
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        Feedback
      </emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(a(1)=11\) and \(a(n)=4a(n-1)\) for \(n\geq2\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: This recursive definition would give a geometric sequence.
          The answer is \(a(1)=11\) and \(a(n)=a(n-1)-4\) for \(n\geq2\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(a(1)=11\) and \(a(n)=a(n-1)-4\) for \(n\geq2\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
      That’s correct! Check yourself: The recursive definition would give an arithmetic sequence, like the
          one that is graphed. The terms are 11, 7, 3, –1, –5.
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       \(a(11)=1\) and \(a(n)=a(n-1)-4\) for \(n\geq2\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
      Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The first point is \((1,11)\), not \((11,1)\). The answer
          is \(a(1)=11\) and \(a(n)=a(n-1)-4\) for \(n\geq2\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(a(1)=11\) and \(a(n)=a(n)-4\) for \(n\geq2\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
       Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: This recursive definition does not account for the previous
          term. The answer is \(a(1)=11\) and \(a(n)=a(n-1)-4\) for \(n\geq2\).
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>4.17.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with
      the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with
      those who could benefit from it. </emphasis></emphasis></para>
<title>Representing Sequences</title>
<para>Let’s look at some more examples for finding the recursive definition of arithmetic and geometric sequences
  ways to represent them.</para>
<para>Example 1:</para>
<para>An arithmetic sequence, \(a\), begins 11, 7, . . .</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    Write a recursive definition for this sequence using function notation.
  </item>
  <item>
    Sketch a graph of the first 5 terms of this sequence.
  </item>
  <item>
    Explain how to use the recursive definition to find \(a(100)\). (Don’t actually determine the value.)
  </item>
</list>
<para>Solutions:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    \(a(1)=11, a(n)=a(n-1)-4, n\geq2\)
  </item>
  <item>
  </item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/5a18ffb1e1cf2d053ce1861f58f826d34d5d7dbf"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    Starting with 11, keep subtracting 4 until you do that 99 times.
  </item>
</list>
<para>Example 2: </para>
<para>A geometric sequence, \(g\), starts 80, 40, . . .</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    Write a recursive definition for this sequence using function notation.
  </item>
  <item>
    Use your definition to make a table of values for \(g(n)\) for the first 6 terms.
  </item>
  <item>
    Explain how to use the recursive definition to find \(g(100)\). (Don’t actually determine the value.)
  </item>
</list>
<para>Solutions:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    \(g(1)=80, g(n)= \frac12 \cdot g(n-1), n\geq2\)
  </item>
  <item>
  </item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">

  <thead/>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        \(n\)
      </emphasis></entry><entry>
       1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry>
        5
      </entry>
      <entry>
        6
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        \(g(n)\)
      </emphasis></entry><entry>
        80
      </entry>
      <entry>
        40
      </entry>
      <entry>
        20
      </entry>
      <entry>
        10
      </entry>
      <entry>
       5
      </entry>
      <entry>
        2.5
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    Starting with 80, keep dividing by 2 until you do that 99 times.
  </item>
</list>
<title>Try It: Representing Sequences</title>
<para>Write a recursive definition for \(P(n)\), the sequence below:</para>
<para>1, 2, 4, 8, 16</para>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to write the recursive definition for \(P(n)\):</para>
<para>First: \(P(1)=1\), then each term is 2 times the previous term, so \(P(n)=2P(n-1), n\geq2\)</para></section>
